## PR Checklist

- [x] The commit message follows our guidelines: https://github.com/angular/angular-cli/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md#-commit-message-guidelines
- [x] Tests for the changes have been added (for bug fixes / features)
- [ ] Docs have been added / updated (for bug fixes / features)

## PR Type

- [x] Bugfix

## What is the current behavior?

Issue Number: #34443

The unit-test builder forces `fsModuleCache: false` (f0a74e8f0), because Vitest keys its module cache on the file found on disk while a test entry point is served from its bundle: changing a file imported by a spec did not change the key, and the cache served a stale bundle. As a result the cache can never be used, even when enabled in the `runnerConfig`.

## What is the new behavior?

The `angular:test-in-memory-provider` plugin defines a cache key generator in `configureVitest`: for a module it serves, the key includes the content hash of the build output file and of its source map, so it changes whenever the served code changes. Modules served from a disk file bail out of the cache; modules the plugin does not serve keep Vitest's default key. `fsModuleCache` is no longer forced off, and the output path computation is shared between `load()` and the key generator.

The generator is registered through `defineCacheKeyGenerator` on Vitest 5, and through `experimental_defineCacheKeyGenerator` on Vitest 4.1, which only has that name. Vitest 4.0 has no file system module cache, so no generator is registered there and nothing changes.

With deterministic test bundles (#34337), an unchanged build gives the same keys, so the cache is reused across runs.

Tests:
- the stale-bundle test from 5e0fd4f13 still checks that changing an imported file is picked up, now with the cache directory created;
- a new test checks that a second run without changes writes no new cache entry.

## How Vitest builds the key

From Vitest 5.0.5, `packages/vitest/src/node/cache/fsModuleCache.ts` (`generateCachePath`, trimmed):

```ts
let hashString = ''

for (const generator of this.fsCacheKeyGenerators.get(vitestConfig) || []) {
  const result = generator({ environment, id, sourceCode: fileContent })
  if (typeof result === 'string') {
    hashString += result
  }
  if (result === false) {
    this.saveMemoryCache(environment, id, null)
    return null
  }
}
// ...then hashed together with the environment config and `fileContent`
```

`fileContent` is the file read from disk for `id`. For a spec, that is the `.spec.ts` source, while the module actually served is its bundle, which also contains the application code the spec imports. That is the stale-bundle case behind f0a74e8f0. The generator's return value maps to:

| Returned | Effect | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| a string | appended to the key | a module served from memory: `<output hash>:<source map hash>` |
| `false` | the module is never cached | a module served from a disk file (`origin: 'disk'` has no hash) |
| `undefined` | Vitest's default key | a module the plugin does not serve |

## Example

A project opting in through its `runnerConfig`:

```ts
// vitest.config.mts
import { defineConfig } from 'vitest/config';

export default defineConfig({
  test: {
    fsModuleCache: true,
    fsModuleCachePath: './.vitest-cache',
  },
});
```

```ts
// src/app/value.ts
export const VALUE = 'initial';

// src/app/app.component.spec.ts
import { expect, test } from 'vitest';
import { VALUE } from './value';

test('uses the value from the imported module', () => {
  expect(VALUE).toBe('initial');
});
```

- Changing `value.ts` without touching the spec: the spec's bundle hash changes, so does its key, and the new code runs. Before f0a74e8f0 this served the old bundle.
- Running again with nothing changed: same bundles, same hashes, same keys, so the entries from the previous run are read back and no new entry is written.

## Does this PR introduce a breaking change?

- [ ] Yes
- [x] No

## Other information

Measured on an application with 255 spec files (numbers in #34443): Vitest duration from ~21.4 s to ~18.4–20 s with a warm cache. Changing a file imported by a spec, without touching the spec, makes that spec fail as expected; reverting it reuses the original cache entry. Watch mode is unaffected: `invalidateFile` already drops the memoized cache key of the invalidated modules.

